        <h3>Bird Watching in Amaltari</h3>
        <p>
            Amaltari, located near the Chitwan National Park, is a haven for bird watchers and nature enthusiasts. The region's diverse habitats including forests, riverbanks, and wetlands make it an ideal spot for observing a wide variety of bird species in their natural environment.
        </p>

        <h3>Popular Bird Species</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Greater Adjutant Stork</li>
            <li>Kingfishers</li>
            <li>Asian Paradise Flycatcher</li>
            <li>Bar-headed Goose</li>
            <li>Hornbills</li>
            <li>Various species of eagles, owls, and woodpeckers</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Timing & Best Seasons</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Best Time of Day:</strong> Early mornings (5:30 AM – 9:00 AM) and late afternoons (3:30 PM – 6:00 PM)</li>
            <li><strong>Best Seasons:</strong> October to April when migratory birds are abundant</li>
            <li><strong>Duration:</strong> Usually 2 to 3 hours per session</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>What to Expect</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Guided walks along forest trails and riverbanks with expert naturalists</li>
            <li>Use of binoculars and bird identification guides</li>
            <li>Opportunities to spot both resident and migratory birds</li>
            <li>Quiet, peaceful environment perfect for photography and nature observation</li>
            <li>Chance to learn about bird behavior, habitat, and conservation efforts</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Bird Watching Routes</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Riverside Trails:</strong> Follow the Narayani Riverbank, a hotspot for waterbirds and kingfishers.</li>
            <li><strong>Forest Pathways:</strong> Walk through mixed deciduous forests to spot hornbills, owls, and woodpeckers.</li>
            <li><strong>Wetlands and Grasslands:</strong> Explore nearby wetlands to observe migratory species such as the bar-headed goose and various ducks.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Interesting Facts</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Chitwan National Park is home to over 500 species of birds, many of which can be spotted around Amaltari.</li>
            <li>The Greater Adjutant Stork, once critically endangered, has a thriving population here due to conservation efforts.</li>
            <li>Bird watching tours help support local conservation and eco-tourism initiatives.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Additional Tips</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Wear comfortable walking shoes and light, neutral-colored clothing</li>
            <li>Bring a hat, sunscreen, and insect repellent</li>
            <li>Maintain silence and avoid sudden movements to increase chances of sightings</li>
            <li>Respect wildlife and their habitats</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>



